.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-APPLY-PLATFORM-HOLDS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-apply-platform-holds
.Nd pin the kernel, ZFS, boot chain and NVIDIA packages so that no update moves them
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
ZFS and the NVIDIA driver are modules built for one kernel.
An update that brings a new kernel, and a module build that then fails,
leaves a machine that cannot mount its pool at the next boot.
So kldload lets userspace take its security updates and pins the
packages that are coupled to the kernel.
.Nm
finds the ones installed and pins them with the package manager's own
mechanism:
.Bl -tag -width "pacman"
.It apt
.Ql apt-mark hold ,
for the Debian and Ubuntu kernel meta-packages, ZFS, GRUB, shim and
mokutil, and every NVIDIA package.
dpkg does not wait for its lock, so while any hold is missing it tries
again every 5 seconds, for up to 5 minutes.
.It dnf
.Ql dnf versionlock ,
for every package named kernel*, the ZFS libraries, GRUB, shim, mokutil
and NVIDIA.
Where the versionlock plugin is not installed it adds the names to
.Ql exclude=
in
.Pa /etc/dnf/dnf.conf
instead, and says so: an excluded package cannot be installed at all,
which is more than a pin.
.It pacman
.Ql IgnorePkg
in
.Pa /etc/pacman.conf .
.El
.Pp
The NVIDIA container toolkit is left out, since it follows its own
releases, not the driver's; so are
.Ql kernel-srpm-macros
and
.Ql zfs-release ,
the repository definition.
.Pp
It then reads the pins back from the package manager, writes those it
found to
.Pa /var/lib/kldload/platform-holds.list ,
and fails if any package it asked for is not among them.
The list is what the web console and
.Xr kldload-rollback 8
read to say whether the machine is pinned.
.Pa /var/lib/kldload
is its own dataset on a kldload install; the list is not written until
it is mounted there.
.Pp
.Pa kldload-package-holds.service
runs it at every boot, so a package installed since is pinned at the
next.
Running it again changes nothing already pinned.
It needs root.
.Pp
To move the kernel on purpose: unpin, update, check with
.Ql dkms status
that ZFS and NVIDIA built for the new kernel, and run
.Nm
again.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the header comment and exit 0, pinning nothing.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_STATE_DATASET
The dataset that must be mounted on
.Pa /var/lib/kldload
before the list is written; default
.Ql rpool/kldload/state .
Where it does not exist the check is skipped.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/platform-holds.list
One package name a line: what is pinned.
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/platform-holds.log
Each run: the package manager's own output, the mechanism and how to
undo it, the count pinned, and anything that was not.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Everything asked for is pinned; or there was nothing to pin; or there is
no apt, dnf or pacman, which is warned on standard error.
.It 1
A platform package is not pinned, named on standard error; or the state
dataset could not be mounted.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wc -l /var/lib/kldload/platform-holds.list
33 /var/lib/kldload/platform-holds.list
$ head -5 /var/lib/kldload/platform-holds.list
akmod-nvidia
grub2-common
grub2-efi-x64
grub2-tools
grub2-tools-minimal
# (exited 0)
$ dnf versionlock list | grep -A1 "^Package name: kernel-core$"
Package name: kernel-core
evr = 7.2.2-300.fc44
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
In a Debian 13 container with
.Ql mokutil
installed, run twice:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-apply-platform-holds
# (exited 0)
# kldload-apply-platform-holds
# (exited 0)
# apt-mark showhold
mokutil
# (exited 0)
# cat /var/log/kldload/platform-holds.log
mokutil set on hold.
2026-09-30T09:47:11 unpin with: apt-mark unhold <pkg>
2026-09-30T09:47:11 [apt] pinned 1 package(s)
mokutil was already set on hold.
2026-09-30T09:47:11 unpin with: apt-mark unhold <pkg>
2026-09-30T09:47:11 [apt] pinned 1 package(s)
.Ed
.Pp
In a Fedora 44 container with
.Ql mokutil
installed, and
.Xr dnf 8
replaced by a script that ignores
.Ql versionlock add
and so pins nothing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-apply-platform-holds
kldload: WARNING — these platform packages were not pinned: mokutil
kldload: an upgrade could move them independently; see /var/log/kldload/platform-holds.log
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pacman.conf 5 ,
.Xr apt-mark 8 ,
.Xr dnf 8 ,
.Xr kldload-rollback 8
.Sh HISTORY
Through 1.4.0-rc14 it pinned on apt only, so Fedora and RHEL installs had
no pins at all (2026-08-17).
On 2026-08-16 it tried to hold every Ubuntu kernel name on a Debian
machine; on 2026-08-27 it stopped seeing the packages it had already
held, found nothing to pin, and emptied the list.
On 2026-08-28 the list was written before its dataset mounted, and the
mount hid it; again on 2026-09-15.
On 2026-09-28 a first boot pinned none of its packages because dpkg was
busy, and reported success.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
On a dnf system
.Xr kldload-firstboot 8
adds versionlocks of its own, from a fixed list that includes the eBPF
tools and NVIDIA package names that may not be installed.
Those are not in the list; the machine above has 49 locks and 33 listed,
and
.Ql nvidia-driver ,
not installed, locked at two versions.
